speechtext:
1. In so far as the Department of External Affairs is concerned, the piano provided Admiral Falls' official residence in Brussels was purchased from Yamaha Canada Music Ltd. of Scarborough, Ontario at a cost of $4,057, with a promised delivery of three months. The piano was manufactured in Japan.
2. At the time of purchase the government was not aware that the Heintzman Company was closing down. However, they were asked to submit a quotation on the piano. The Heintzman Company confirmed that the smallest grand piano manufactured by them would cost $7,900 and the delivery would be six to eight months. The decision to purchase the Yamaha piano rather than the Heintzman piano was based on these factors:
(a)	The cost differential of almost 100 per cent;
(b)	The smaller size of the Yamaha piano could be better accommodated in Admiral Falls' house;
(c)	The earlier delivery promise of Yamaha permitted us to consolidate its shipment with the other Canadian manufactured furniture, thus resulting in a significant saving in its shipping cost.
3. (a) and (b) Admiral Falls does play the piano; however, the provision of the piano was based on representational requirements not the personal factors. Representational functions at which the piano is to be used include recitals of visiting Canadian artists and social functions.

Yes.
(a)	On or about November 22, 1978;
(b)	258,525 copies, $65,029 (including $6,750 federal sales tax);
(c)	The brochure was distributed to Transport Canada headquarters and to the six regional offices of Transport Canada (Air) to be used in a potential market of approximately 250,000 persons, both internal and external to Transport Canada. The market was seen to be the general public, specifically the air passengers; the aviation industry; the 650 (approx.) licensed air carriers in Canada; pilots and air crew; aviation associations; suppliers; provincial aviation councils and their support employees;
(d)	To describe the role of the Canadian Air Transportation administration.
